{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-00d8186a-1247-4fb3-b93d-afe97b913dc3","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-9847480610cd0d41","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-9020-djmhh0e4/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9020-E001.md","content":"# AMD Instinct validation campaign: vLLM ROCm functional and performance parity\n\n## Description\n\nThis epic resolves the platform's lack of first-hand ROCm data by running a validation campaign on physical AMD Instinct accelerators. It produces functional and performance parity data for vLLM serving of the supported model set (ROCm vs. the shipped CUDA builds) and a go/no-go recommendation that defines the scope of vLLM ROCm runtime productization. The campaign also performs first-time environment bring-up of the AMD software stack (ROCm driver/runtime, GPU device plugin exposing `amd.com/gpu`) on OpenShift, since no AMD hardware exists in the CI fleet today.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Bring up the AMD software stack (ROCm driver and runtime, GPU device plugin / GPU operator exposing `amd.com/gpu` resources) on the AMD Instinct nodes and target OpenShift version.\n- Drive resolution of hardware access (procurement or partner-lab engagement) — the open access path is tracked here as scope and risk; access being granted is externally controlled and is not an acceptance criterion.\n- For each model in the supported model set, run vLLM ROCm serving and collect:\n  - functional correctness results (does the model serve correctly on ROCm),\n  - performance parity data against the shipped CUDA builds of the same runtime (throughput, latency).\n- Compile results into a validation report stating, per model, whether ROCm serving meets the functional and performance bar, and a go/no-go recommendation with the proposed productization scope.\n- Track alignment (or misalignment) of upstream vLLM ROCm image releases with our release trains and record the findings in the report.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- The AMD software stack is running on the AMD Instinct environment and `amd.com/gpu` resources are discoverable by workloads on the target OpenShift version.\n- A validation report exists covering every model in the supported model set, with per-model functional correctness results and performance measurements against the CUDA baseline.\n- The report states a go/no-go recommendation and an explicit list of models cleared for productization.\n- Upstream ROCm image cadence observations relevant to release-train alignment are recorded in the report.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-1 (P0): A validation campaign on physical AMD Instinct hardware establishes functional and performance parity data for vLLM serving of the supported model set, producing the go/no-go scope for productization.\n\n## Risks and Notes\n\n- Hardware access is unresolved: dedicated AMD Instinct nodes require procurement or partner-lab access. The campaign's start and duration are exposed to externally controlled procurement and lab schedules; securing access is part of this epic's work, but access being granted is not a condition this epic's deliverable can produce.\n- Upstream ROCm image release cadence is controlled by AMD and the upstream communities and may not align with our release trains.\n- Performance parity thresholds for the go/no-go call involve product judgment; the epic produces the data and a recommendation, and the productization decision is confirmed with product stakeholders.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":38764,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b8cd0f36882c8f34","agent_id":"a10cfa25a5e863fb5","session_id":"bc9ea267-6fda-4bac-95a7-007b50b2b12c","uuid":"d5fa4a3e-9bae-4b33-a16d-63d7efd12e4d","timestamp":"2026-10-23T19:07:21.577Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Dec