{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9a3a533f5cf30141","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9020-E001\n3\ttitle: 'AMD Instinct validation campaign: vLLM ROCm functional and performance parity'\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9020\n5\tcomponent: Serving Runtimes\n6\tteam: RHOAI Serving\n7\ttype: Investigation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies: []\n10\tinvestigation_signals:\n11\t  question_specificity: 1\n12\t  source_accessibility: 0\n13\t  local_runnability: 0\n14\t  cluster_hardware_dependence: -2\n15\t  human_judgment_required: -1\n16\timplementation_type: null\n17\tai_signals: null\n18\tai_implementability: Low\n19\tai_implementability_score: -2\n20\tjira_key: null\n21\tbranch: null\n22\tgated_by: null\n23\tgate_failure_impact: null\n24\t---\n25\t# AMD Instinct validation campaign: vLLM ROCm functional and performance parity\n26\t\n27\t## Description\n28\t\n29\tThis epic resolves the platform's lack of first-hand ROCm data by running a validation campaign on physical AMD Instinct accelerators. It produces functional and performance parity data for vLLM serving of the supported model set (ROCm vs. the shipped CUDA builds) and a go/no-go recommendation that defines the scope of vLLM ROCm runtime productization. The campaign also performs first-time environment bring-up of the AMD software stack (ROCm driver/runtime, GPU device plugin exposing `amd.com/gpu`) on OpenShift, since no AMD hardware exists in the CI fleet today.\n30\t\n31\t## Scope\n32\t\n33\t- Bring up the AMD software stack (ROCm driver and runtime, GPU device plugin / GPU operator exposing `amd.com/gpu` resources) on the AMD Instinct nodes and target OpenShift version.\n34\t- Drive resolution of hardware access (procurement or partner-lab engagement) — the open access path is tracked here as scope and risk; access being granted is externally controlled and is not an acceptance criterion.\n35\t- For each model in the supported model set, run vLLM ROCm serving and collect:\n36\t  - functional correctness results (does the model serve correctly on ROCm),\n37\t  - performance parity data against the shipped CUDA builds of the same runtime (throughput, latency).\n38\t- Compile results into a validation report stating, per model, whether ROCm serving meets the functional and performance bar, and a go/no-go recommendation with the proposed productization scope.\n39\t- Track alignment (or misalignment) of upstream vLLM ROCm image releases with our release trains and record the findings in the report.\n40\t\n41\t## Acceptance Criteria\n42\t\n43\t- The AMD software stack is running on the AMD Instinct environment and `amd.com/gpu` resources are discoverable by workloads on the target OpenShift version.\n44\t- A validation report exists covering every model in the supported model set, with per-model functional correctness results and performance measurements against the CUDA baseline.\n45\t- The report states a go/no-go recommendation and an explicit list of models cleared for productization.\n46\t- Upstream ROCm image cadence observations relevant to release-train alignment are recorded in the report.\n47\t\n48\t## HLR Traceability\n49\t\n50\t- HLR-1 (P0): A validation campaign on physical AMD Instinct hardware establishes functional and performance parity data for vLLM serving of the supported model set, producing the go/no-go scope for productization.\n51\t\n52\t## Risks and Notes\n53\t\n54\t- Hardware access is unresolved: dedicated AMD Instinct nodes require procurement or partner-lab access. The campaign's start and duration are exposed to externally controlled procurement and lab schedules; securing access is part of this epic's work, but access being granted is not a condition this epic's deliverable can produce.\n55\t- Upstream ROCm image release cadence is controlled by AMD and the upstream communities and may not align with our release trains.\n56\t- Performance parity thresholds for the go/no-go call involve product judgment; the epic produces the data and a recommendation, and the productization decision is confirmed with product stakeholders.\n57\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8a50c77b23f0e55d","agent_id":"adffe34affbaa2eb0","session_id":"bc9ea267-6fda-4bac-95a7-007b50b2b12c","uuid":"41598872-33b4-405e-9e4b-4f77f545e897","timestamp":"2026-10-23T19:10:24.574Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-9a3a533f5cf30141","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9020 decomposition"}
