{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9945c52b6dbe2463","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9020\n3\tepic_count: 5\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\trevised: true\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9020 (AMD GPU / ROCm enablement for serving and training)\n11\t\n12\t## Epic List\n13\t\n14\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n15\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n16\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E001 | AMD Instinct validation campaign: vLLM ROCm functional and performance parity | Investigation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n17\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E002 | Productize supported vLLM ROCm serving runtime for campaign-cleared models | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n18\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E003 | PyTorch ROCm training support for single- and multi-GPU PyTorchJobs | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P1 |\n19\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E004 | Kueue amd.com/gpu quota handling for ROCm workloads | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P1 |\n20\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E005 | Document supported AMD hardware, driver prerequisites, and validated model list | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Docs | P1 |\n21\t\n22\t## Dependency DAG\n23\t\n24\t```mermaid\n25\tgraph TD\n26\t    E001[\"E001 ROCm parity validation campaign (Investigation)\"] --> E002[\"E002 vLLM ROCm runtime productization\"]\n27\t    E001 --> E005[\"E005 AMD/ROCm documentation\"]\n28\t    E002 --> E005\n29\t    E003[\"E003 PyTorchJob ROCm training support\"] --> E005\n30\t    E004[\"E004 Kueue amd.com/gpu quota handling\"] --> E005\n31\t```\n32\t\n33\t## DAG Justification\n34\t\n35\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n36\t|------|------|-----------|\n37\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The campaign's go/no-go outcome determines which models the productized ROCm runtime supports — it rewrites E002's scope. True gate recorded via `gated_by` with `gate_failure_impact.action=rewrite`: productize only the cleared subset; if no model achieves parity, E002 is dropped and the results feed a follow-up strategy. Bounded outcomes (clear-all / clear-subset / no-go) all map to the same epic with different scope, so no conditional branch files are emitted — the gate is captured in frontmatter, not by duplicating the same productization surface per branch. |\n38\t| E001 → E005 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The documentation's validated model list is a direct deliverable of the campaign, and the campaign's go/no-go outcome changes what E005 documents (on a no-go, the productized-runtime content falls away). True gate recorded via `gated_by` with `gate_failure_impact.action=rewrite`: on a no-go, E005 is rewritten to document the campaign outcome (parity findings, training/quota usage) with no validated model list. E005 is additionally blocked by every Implementation epic below. |\n39\t| E002 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Docs describe the productized runtime. |\n40\t| E003 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Docs describe the supported training configuration. |\n41\t| E004 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Docs describe quota setup for amd.com/gpu. |\n42\t| E003 ∥ E004 | Rule 14 (different repos, no shared artifacts) | Training Operator (opendatahub-io/training-operator) and Kueue (opendatahub-io/kueue) are separate midstream repos. Quota handling for the `amd.com/gpu` resource name is a Kueue-side configuration/coverage change; PyTorchJob ROCm enablement is a separate surface. The end-to-end \"jobs admitted through Kueue with amd.com/gpu quota\" capability is an acceptance criterion on E003, not a DAG edge — the two epics never build the same surface. |\n43\t| E001/E002 ∥ E003/E004 | Rule 17 (parallel work streams) | HLR-3 training enablement is not gated by the serving parity campaign (HLR-1/HLR-2 scope vLLM serving only); training work delivers independent partial value and proceeds on currently published upstream ROCm PyTorch images. |\n44\t\n45\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n46\t\n47\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n48\t|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n49\t| HLR-1 | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9020-E001 | Validation campaign on physical AMD Instinct hardware; deliverable is the parity report plus go/no-go productization scope. |\n50\t| HLR-2 | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9020-E002 | Productization of the vLLM ROCm serving runtime for campaign-cleared models; gated by E001 (`gated_by`, `gate_failure_impact.action=rewrite`). |\n51\t| HLR-3 | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9020-E003, RHAISTRAT-9020-E004 | Split by component boundary (Training Operator vs. Distributed Workloads), not by priority — both halves are P1 and jointly deliver the HLR. |\n52\t| HLR-4 | P2 | RHAISTRAT-9020-E005 | Single docs epic covering all documentation topics. Priority set to P1 per the docs-authoring priority exception (strategy frontmatter `priority: Major` → P1); the P2 label on HLR-4 reads as relative value, not a deferral signal. Gated by E001 (`gated_by`, `gate_failure_impact.action=rewrite`): on a campaign no-go, the docs are rewritten to cover the campaign outcome and training/quota usage with no validated model list. |\n53\t\n54\tFull coverage: HLR-1..HLR-4 each map to at least one epic; no orphaned requirements.\n55\t\n56\t## Health Warnings\n57\t\n58\t- **Missing strategy sections** (non-blocking): the strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, and no NFR section; the Affected Components section is a bare list without change/owner columns. Teams were inferred from `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/` owning-team fields (RHOAI Serving, Distributed Workloads); the Documentation epic is assigned to a docs team by convention since no owner is stated.\n59\t- **Hardware access (Open Question 1) is an availability precondition, not an AC or a separate epic**: dedicated AMD Instinct nodes do not exist in the CI fleet and procurement/partner-lab access is unresolved. Securing access is scope on E001; the *granting* of access is externally controlled and stays in E001's Risks. The campaign's timing is exposed to procurement/partner-lab schedules — a schedule risk, not a decomposition defect. The answer (procured vs. partner-lab nodes) does not change which epics exist or their scope, so it does not warrant an Investigation epic.\n60\t- **Upstream ROCm build cadence (Open Question 2) is an external timing risk**: AMD's runtime image release cadence is outside our control and may not align with our release trains. This gates timing of E002's release alignment, not its existence. See Tiered Delivery below.\n61\t- **AMD GPU Operator is not in the platform inventory** (Rule 22 consideration): architecture context shows all four components depend on the NVIDIA GPU Operator for accelerator discovery; `amd.com/gpu` resource discovery on nodes requires the AMD GPU Operator / ROCm driver stack, which has no architecture-context file and is not a RHAI component. Environment bring-up and validation of the AMD software stack (driver, ROCm runtime, device plugin exposing `amd.com/gpu`) is folded into E001's campaign scope; provisioning it on target clusters is cluster-infrastructure work that rides with the hardware-access resolution. E003/E004 assume `amd.com/gpu` resources are exposed on nodes — validated by the campaign.\n62\t- **KServe is a passive component**: the strategy lists KServe as affected, but no KServe code change is required — the ServingRuntime/InferenceService contract is runtime-agnostic. Validation (\"vLLM ROCm runtime serves under KServe InferenceServices on AMD GPU nodes\") is an acceptance criterion on E002 (nearest active component, same owning team per the architecture context), so no separate KServe validation epic is generated.\n63\t- **Priority notes**: E001 inherits P0 because it blocks all P0 productization work (HLR-2). E005 is P1 via the docs-authoring exception (strategy priority Major → P1) even though HLR-4 is labeled P2 — docs-authoring epics do not push priority upstream and derive it from strategy priority.\n64\t- **Epic sizing**: E001 is the largest epic (environment bring-up + per-model functional and performance validation across the supported model set). It is kept as one logical change because splitting the campaign would fragment the shared hardware environment and report; re-split trigger: split per model family if the campaign outgrows a sprint of effort or if hardware access arrives in tranches.\n65\t\n66\t## Tiered Delivery\n67\t\n68\tThe external dependency (upstream ROCm image cadence, AMD-controlled) has uncertain timing; partial value is deliverable without release-train alignment:\n69\t\n70\t- **Tier 1 (independent, proceed now)**: E001 (parity data and go/no-go scope — risk-reduction value on whatever upstream images are currently published), E003 and E004 (training enablement on currently available upstream ROCm PyTorch images).\n71\t- **Tier 2 (dependency-gated)**: E002 (productized runtime whose release alignment is subject to upstream ROCm image cadence in addition to the campaign's go/no-go), followed by E005 (documents the as-shipped state).\n72\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a13b7f549186121c","agent_id":"a6edeccbbb27ae4d1","session_id":"bc9ea267-6fda-4bac-95a7-007b50b2b12c","uuid":"9b2739fe-4f86-4905-9d3e-3fc8277e569d","timestamp":"2026-10-23T19:15:46.156Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-9945c52b6dbe2463","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-9020"}
