{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9b6a43e507c4fd7d","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9020-E002\n3\ttitle: Productize supported vLLM ROCm serving runtime for campaign-cleared models\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9020\n5\tcomponent: Serving Runtimes\n6\tteam: RHOAI Serving\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies:\n10\t- RHAISTRAT-9020-E001\n11\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-9020-E001\n12\tgate_failure_impact:\n13\t  action: rewrite\n14\t  fallback_approach: Productize only the subset of models the validation campaign\n15\t    clears; if no model achieves functional and performance parity, drop the productization\n16\t    epic and feed the campaign results into a follow-up strategy.\n17\tai_signals:\n18\t  change_specificity: 0\n19\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n20\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n21\t  existing_foundation: 1\n22\t  open_questions: -1\n23\t  external_dependency: -1\n24\t  human_process_gates: -1\n25\t  repo_access: 1\n26\t  architecture_claims: 1\n27\timplementation_type: null\n28\tinvestigation_signals: null\n29\tai_implementability: Medium\n30\tai_implementability_score: 2\n31\tjira_key: null\n32\tbranch: null\n33\t---\n34\t# Productize supported vLLM ROCm serving runtime for campaign-cleared models\n35\t\n36\t## Description\n37\t\n38\tThis epic productizes a supported vLLM ROCm serving runtime for the models cleared by the AMD Instinct validation campaign. It turns the validated ROCm builds into a productized runtime image and serving runtime definition that ships alongside the existing CUDA builds, so RHOAI can serve the cleared model set on AMD Instinct accelerators under KServe InferenceServices.\n39\t\n40\t## Scope\n41\t\n42\t- Build and release the vLLM ROCm runtime image in the existing vLLM midstream build and release pipeline, for the model set cleared by the validation campaign.\n43\t- Define/extend the serving runtime definition (ServingRuntime/ClusterServingRuntime) for the ROCm vLLM image, requesting `amd.com/gpu` resources, alongside the existing CUDA variants.\n44\t- Ensure the runtime's release alignment with the platform release train, using the upstream ROCm image cadence findings from the validation campaign.\n45\t- Declare the support scope (supported models, driver prerequisites) for the productized runtime.\n46\t\n47\t## Acceptance Criteria\n48\t\n49\t- A productized vLLM ROCm runtime image is built and released through the platform's build pipeline with a green pipeline run.\n50\t- A serving runtime definition exists that deploys the ROCm vLLM image, and the runtime successfully serves each campaign-cleared model under KServe InferenceServices on AMD GPU nodes (serverless and/or raw deployment mode as supported).\n51\t- The runtime image and definition are included in a platform release, with the supported model list and driver prerequisites stated.\n52\t- The support scope matches the go/no-go list from the validation campaign — no model is declared supported without campaign clearance.\n53\t\n54\t## HLR Traceability\n55\t\n56\t- HLR-2 (P0): A supported vLLM ROCm serving runtime is productized for the models the validation campaign clears.\n57\t\n58\t## Risks and Notes\n59\t\n60\t- Scope is gated by the validation campaign's go/no-go outcome: productize only the cleared subset; if no model achieves parity, this epic is dropped and the campaign results feed a follow-up strategy.\n61\t- Upstream ROCm image release cadence is controlled by AMD and may affect release-train alignment; misalignment is a timing risk, not a scope change.\n62\t- This runtime is additive alongside the existing CUDA builds — it does not replace an existing capability, so no rollback/feature-flag path is required beyond standard release practices.\n63\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a13b7f549186121c","agent_id":"a6edeccbbb27ae4d1","session_id":"bc9ea267-6fda-4bac-95a7-007b50b2b12c","uuid":"0f420550-d5c9-4a01-a0c8-8e0dabd2655e","timestamp":"